hyph_en_US.dic.gz
    US English hyphenation patterns, hyph_en_US.dic version 2011-10-07
    (conversion and modifications by László Németh, originally distributed
    with the Hyphen hyphenation library), based on the Plain TeX hyphen.tex
    patterns and the TUGboat US English hyphenation-exception log. Shipped
    gzipped and otherwise unchanged; readability.py uses it only to count the
    syllables of words the dictionary lacks. Full upstream README:
    data/LICENSE_syllables.md.

        hyph_en_US.dic: BSD-style. Unlimited copying, redistribution and
        modification of this file is permitted with this copyright and
        license information.

        hyphen.tex: The Plain TeX hyphenation tables [NOT TO BE CHANGED IN
        ANY WAY!] Unlimited copying and redistribution of this file are
        permitted as long as this file is not modified. Modifications are
        permitted, but only if the resulting file is not named hyphen.tex.

        TUGboat exceptions: Copyright 2007 TeX Users Group. You may freely
        use, modify and/or distribute this file.

zipf_en.bin.gz
    Word frequencies on the Zipf scale (log10 frequency per billion words;
    van Heuven et al. 2014) for 147,216 lowercase words, each seen at least 5
    times. Counts only; no running text. Details: data/LICENSE_zipf_en.md.
    Build scripts: tools/build_zipf_table.py, tools/pack_tables.py.

    Licence: CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
    Counted from, with each source normalised and weighted equally:

      - Common Pile v0.1 news_filtered, only articles marked CC BY 4.0 (CC BY-SA
        articles skipped).
      - English Wikinews, snapshot of 2023-07-28. CC BY 2.5.
      - Common Pile v0.1 foodista_filtered (Foodista). CC BY 3.0.
      - Common Pile v0.1 project_gutenberg_filtered, a sample of public-domain
        books.
      - Common Pile v0.1 youtube_filtered, first shard, only transcripts marked
        CC BY 4.0.

    Attribution: "Word counts derived from the Common Pile (EleutherAI et al.,
    2025): news_filtered, foodista_filtered, project_gutenberg_filtered and
    youtube_filtered; and from English Wikinews (Wikimedia Foundation,
    CC BY 2.5)." Paragraphs that also appear in proseweave's open validation
    corpus were left out of the counts.

mag_news_freq.bin.xz
    Word and phrase frequency tables for news and magazine-style writing:
    lemma unigrams, lemma n-grams (2-4 words) and word-class-masked n-grams,
    with counts. No running text is stored. Details:
    data/LICENSE_mag_news_freq.md. Build script: tools/build_mag_news_freq.py.

    Licence: CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
    Counted from:

      - Common Pile v0.1 news_filtered (Kandpal et al., 2025, "The Common Pile
        v0.1"): Global Voices and the other news sites collected there, only
        the articles published under CC BY 4.0 (CC BY-SA articles skipped).
      - English Wikinews, snapshot of 2023-07-28. CC BY 2.5.
      - Common Pile v0.1 foodista_filtered (Foodista). CC BY 3.0.
      - Common Pile v0.1 project_gutenberg_filtered, a 10M-word sample of books
        marked public domain.

    Counted from scratch from the open sources above. Not derived from COCA or
    from any other tool's frequency lists.

    Attribution: "Word and phrase counts derived from Global Voices, Wikinews,
    Foodista, the news sites collected in Common Pile v0.1 (Kandpal et al.,
    2025, The Common Pile v0.1), and Project Gutenberg texts, used under
    CC BY 4.0, CC BY 3.0, CC BY 2.5 and public domain terms respectively."

    Tokens, word classes and lemmas for the build came from proseweave's own
    bundled parser and tagger rules (see syntax_en.bin.gz above), at build
    time, without Jev.

similarity_glove.bin.gz, similarity_wiki.bin.gz, similarity_sent.bin.gz,
similarity_routes.json
    Semantic spaces for the similarity indices (similarity.py): word vectors
    only, 4-bit quantised; no running text is stored. Details:
    data/SIMILARITY_NOTICE.md. Each licence has its own file, so share-alike
    terms apply only to the file whose inputs carry them.

    similarity_glove.bin.gz - GloVe 6B 100d word vectors: Jeffrey Pennington,
        Richard Socher and Christopher D. Manning (2014), "GloVe: Global
        Vectors for Word Representation", EMNLP. Made available under the
        Open Data Commons Public Domain Dedication and License (PDDL) 1.0,
        https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/pddl/1-0/.

    similarity_wiki.bin.gz - an LSA space and a word2vec space trained on
        WikiText-103: Stephen Merity, Caiming Xiong, James Bradbury and
        Richard Socher (2016), "Pointer Sentinel Mixture Models". The text is
        by the Wikipedia contributors under CC BY-SA 3.0. This file is an
        adaptation of it and is licensed CC BY-SA 3.0
        (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/). The licence covers
        this file only.

    similarity_sent.bin.gz - a static vector for each WordPiece token of
        sentence-transformers' all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (Nils Reimers, Iryna Gurevych
        and the sentence-transformers contributors; revision
        1110a243fdf4706b3f48f1d95db1a4f5529b4d41), plus that model's token
        vocabulary, fitted so that summed sentence vectors reproduce the
        model's sentence similarities. Training used 16.6M public-domain sentences
        only:
          - 1,654 Project Gutenberg books (public domain in the United States; no
            Project Gutenberg header, licence text or trademark is used);
          - the US Congressional Record and congressional hearing
            transcripts, works of the US federal government (17 U.S.C. 105),
            obtained through the Common Pile v0.1 usgpo_filtered collection
            (Kandpal et al. 2025).
        Only fitted token vectors are stored, not the model. Licensed under the Apache
        License, Version 2.0 (http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0),
        the licence of the model; full text in data/LICENSE_Apache-2.0.md:

            Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the "License");
            you may not use this file except in compliance with the License.
            Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing,
            software distributed under the License is distributed on an "AS
            IS" BASIS, WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either
            express or implied. See the License for the specific language
            governing permissions and limitations under the License.

    similarity_routes.json - proseweave's own fitted blend weights and
        calibration constants, MIT like the code.

connectives_en.json
    proseweave's inventory of English connectives, grouped by the relation
    each signals. Compiled independently for proseweave and released under
    proseweave's MIT licence. The category definitions draw on the published
    taxonomies of Halliday & Hasan (1976), Cohesion in English; Quirk,
    Greenbaum, Leech & Svartvik (1985), A Comprehensive Grammar of the English
    Language; Sanders, Spooren & Noordman (1992), "Toward a taxonomy of
    coherence relations"; Louwerse (2001), "An analytic and cognitive
    parametrization of coherence relations"; and Graesser, McNamara, Louwerse
    & Cai (2004), "Coh-Metrix: Analysis of text on cohesion and language". The
    members were chosen from general knowledge of English; no other tool's
    word lists were consulted.

Independence
============

proseweave's indices follow the definitions in the published cohesion research
(Crossley, Kyle & McNamara 2016; Crossley, Kyle & Dascalu 2019; McNamara,
Graesser, McCarthy & Cai 2014, and the other works cited in README.md). Its
outputs were scored against reference outputs (TAACO 2.0 for the cohesion and
source indices, a spaCy-based feature script for the basic features, and an LLM
judge for the easability components; run by us, not redistributed) to check
that each index measures what it should. proseweave
contains none of those tools' code, word lists or data files; its own data files
are listed above. proseweave is independent and not affiliated with or endorsed
by the authors of these tools.
